==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|targon}} (cf. modern {{m|fr|estragon}}), from {{der|en|ML.|tragonia}}, from {{der|en|ar|طَرْخُون}}, ultimately from {{der|en|grc|δρακόντιον|t=[[dragonwort]], ''[[Dracunculus vulgaris]]''}}, from {{m|grc|δράκων|t=dragon, serpent}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA|nMmmm}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtæɹəɡɑn/|/ˈtæɹəɡən/}}
* {{a|GA|Mmmm}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtɛɹəɡɑn/|/ˈtɛɹəɡən/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtæɹəɡən/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-tarragon.wav|Audio (RP)}}
* {{hyphenation|en|tar|ra|gon}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# A [[perennial]] [[herb]], the [[wormwood]] species ''[[Artemisia dracunculus]]'', from [[Europe]] and parts of [[Asia]].
# The [[leaves]] of this [[plant]] (either fresh, or preserved in [[vinegar]] / [[oil]] [[mixture]]) used as a [[seasoning]].

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|estragon}}, {{l|en|dragon's wort}}, {{vern|French tarragon}}, {{vern|Russian tarragon}}, {{vern|silky wormwood}}, {{vern|wild tarragon}}

